WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.340
A newborn mouse that lacks the muscle kinase MuSK cannot breathe.

2
00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:14.140
Its motor axons reach the diaphragm, but no neuromuscular junction forms.

3
00:00:15.140 --> 00:00:18.720
A synapse is not built whenever an axon touches a target.

4
00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:23.880
A healthy junction packs its acetylcholine receptors under the nerve ending.

5
00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:27.520
Where do the receptors go, and who tells them?

6
00:00:28.283 --> 00:00:30.603
The muscle can start the job alone.

7
00:00:31.463 --> 00:00:36.423
In mouse embryos with no motor neurons, the diaphragm still builds a band of

8
00:00:36.423 --> 00:00:38.283
receptor clusters across its middle.

9
00:00:39.063 --> 00:00:41.083
The muscle laid down a prepattern.

10
00:00:41.943 --> 00:00:45.463
That prepattern needs MuSK, but it does not need agrin.

11
00:00:46.443 --> 00:00:51.083
When the nerve does arrive, the band narrows, and only the receptors beneath the

12
00:00:51.083 --> 00:00:52.083
terminal are kept.

13
00:00:53.063 --> 00:00:57.323
Clusters can form without a nerve, but a nerve is still needed for a synapse.

14
00:00:58.167 --> 00:01:00.507
The nerve adds its own signal.

15
00:01:01.147 --> 00:01:06.607
It makes a protein called agrin and sends it into the basal lamina, the thin layer

16
00:01:06.607 --> 00:01:08.087
around each muscle fibre.

17
00:01:09.307 --> 00:01:12.407
Agrin binds Lrp4 on the muscle surface.

18
00:01:13.567 --> 00:01:20.227
Lrp4 forms a complex with MuSK, and Dok7 inside the muscle boosts MuSK.

19
00:01:21.247 --> 00:01:24.547
Downstream of MuSK, rapsyn holds the receptors together.

20
00:01:25.500 --> 00:01:26.760
Here is the puzzle.

21
00:01:27.580 --> 00:01:31.500
The muscle can cluster receptors with no agrin at all.

22
00:01:31.900 --> 00:01:35.580
Yet mice that lack agrin lose most of their clusters.

23
00:01:36.660 --> 00:01:38.820
The nerve brings a second signal.

24
00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:45.460
Acetylcholine, released at every terminal, disperses receptor clusters that agrin

25
00:01:45.460 --> 00:01:46.460
does not protect.

26
00:01:47.250 --> 00:01:49.450
Test it with mutant mice.

27
00:01:50.490 --> 00:01:56.910
Remove agrin, and acetylcholine disperses the unprotected clusters, so most are

28
00:01:56.910 --> 00:02:03.110
lost. Remove the enzyme that makes acetylcholine, and nothing disperses them.

29
00:02:03.650 --> 00:02:07.070
The muscle keeps more clusters, over a wider band.

30
00:02:08.230 --> 00:02:14.250
Now remove both. Clusters return, built by the muscle's own MuSK program.

31
00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:17.820
The double mutant is the telling case.

32
00:02:18.440 --> 00:02:23.560
If agrin's job were to start clusters, losing acetylcholine could not bring them

33
00:02:23.560 --> 00:02:29.840
back. So in the animal, agrin's main job is to protect clusters from dispersal by

34
00:02:29.840 --> 00:02:34.600
acetylcholine. Receptor clustering is a competition between two forces.

35
00:02:35.420 --> 00:02:36.900
Agrin and MuSK facilitate.

36
00:02:37.740 --> 00:02:38.980
Acetylcholine inhibits.

37
00:02:39.880 --> 00:02:44.060
Keep this rule: the muscle starts clusters, and agrin protects them.
